course: dictionary
lesson: 3701
title: Flattery and honest praise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Flattery is praise that is too much or not sincere. People often give it to please someone, or to get something from them. When someone piles on compliments, ask yourself what they want.
explain: Honest praise is different. It is true, and it helps the person who hears it. In Aesop's fable, the fox flatters the crow so that it drops its cheese. The fox's praise is a trick, not a gift.
explain: «Flattery will get you nowhere» is a joking reply to compliments, and it means you do not fall for it. «When a stranger told Zara she was "the best player EVER", she said thanks and kept chatting only in the game.» Big, fast praise from a stranger can be a hook to win your trust.
fact: Flattery is praise that is too much or not sincere.
fact: «Empty flattery» means praise with no real meaning behind it.
fact: In Aesop's fable, the fox flatters the crow so that it drops its cheese.
fact: Honest, kind feedback helps people more than praise said to everything, like "Great question!"
fact: Flattery is not honest feedback, and it is not always true just because it feels nice.
exercise: What is flattery? | Loud shouting at someone | Honest feedback that helps you improve | *Praise that is too much or not sincere, given to please someone or get something | A kind of cheese
exercise: In Aesop's fable, why does the fox flatter the crow? | *To get the crow's cheese | To teach it to sing | To make it laugh | To say sorry for something
exercise: «Flattery will get you nowhere» is a joking reply to what? | A rainy day | *Compliments | Rude shouting | Questions about homework
test: Which one is honest praise? | Praise given only to get a favour | *Kind, true feedback that helps someone improve | Saying «Great question!» to everything, whatever is asked | Telling someone they are smarter than everyone else
test: Why should you be careful when a stranger gives you lots of praise? | Praise is never a problem | It is always true, so trust it straight away | *It could be flattery, a hook to win your trust | It means they are a close friend
exam: Which word means praise that is too much or not sincere? | Feedback | Modesty | Honesty | *Flattery
